<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Althing</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/930">930</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Alþingi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Althing is the parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a>, founded around 930 as an open-air general assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thingvellir">Þingvellir</a>. In the Icelandic Commonwealth it made law and settled disputes, with a law-speaker who recited the laws. It lost its role under Norwegian and Danish rule and was abolished in 1800, but was restored in Reykjavík in 1845 and became the parliament of independent Iceland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/althing.jpg" alt="Althing" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Great Brightstar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Althing_Logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Althing was established as an outdoor assembly, or thing, on the plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thingvellir">Þingvellir</a> from about 930. At first it was the general assembly of the Icelandic Commonwealth, where the most powerful leaders, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothi">goðar</a>, met to decide legislation and dispense justice. All free men could attend, and it was usually the main social event of the year. The centre was the Lögberg, or Law Rock, where the Lawspeaker presided and recited the laws in effect.</p><p>The Gulathing Law was adopted at the first Althing in 930, introduced by Úlfljótr, who had spent three years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> studying its laws. According to Njáls saga, the Althing declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> the official religion in 1000, after Thorgeir Ljosvetningagodi, a pagan chieftain, proposed one law and one religion for the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The legislative section, the Lögrétta, was the most powerful institution. It comprised the 39 district chieftains, nine additional members and the Lawspeaker. After the country was divided into four quarters around 965 a court of 36 judges was set up for each at the Althing. When the Icelanders submitted to the Norwegian king under the Old Covenant of 1262, the Commonwealth ended and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothi">goðar</a> ceased to rule. The Lögrétta, now of 36 members, shared legislative power with the king. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a>, Icelanders gave up the right to initiate and consent to legislation, and the Althing served almost only as a court.</p><p>A royal decree disbanded it in 1800, and a High Court in Reykjavík took over its functions. A decree of 1843 provided for a new Althing, which met in Reykjavík on 1 July 1845 as a consultative assembly of 26 members, elected by only about 5 percent of the population. The Constitution of 1874 gave it joint legislative power with the Crown, and it met from 1881 in the newly built Parliament House. A constitutional amendment confirmed on 3 October 1903 granted home rule. The Act of Union took effect on 1 December 1918, and the Althing declared the republic at a session at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thingvellir">Þingvellir</a> on 17 June 1944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Althing claims to be the longest-running parliament in the world. Its two chambers were merged into one in 1991. The unicameral parliament now has 63 members, elected every four years by party-list proportional representation, and sits in the Alþingishús, built of hewn Icelandic stone in 1881.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Althing” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1000, with the island divided and Norway’s king pressing for conversion, the <b>Althing</b> asked the lawspeaker Thorgeir, himself a pagan, to decide; after lying silent under his cloak for a day and a night, he ruled that all Icelanders should be baptised, but that pagan sacrifice in…</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In Iceland, which had no king, the <b>Althing</b> met each summer at Þingvellir from about 930, with a law-speaker who recited the law.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Althing</b> of Iceland met from about 930, Alfonso IX of León summoned townsmen to his Cortes in 1188, and in England Simon de Montfort’s parliament of 1265 included representatives of the towns; the word comes from the French parler, “to speak”.</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In Iceland the choice was made by law: around 1000 the <b>Althing</b> asked the lawspeaker Thorgeir, himself a pagan, to decide, and he ruled that all Icelanders should be baptised but that pagan sacrifice in private would be tolerated for a time.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li><li><blockquote>Every free farmer of means had to follow a goði of his choice, who backed him in lawsuits and could call on him for armed support, and the goðar ran the local assemblies, sat in the law council of the <b>Althing</b> and named its judges.</blockquote><a href="/societies/vikings">Social Hierarchy of the Vikings</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 930 they founded the <b>Althing</b>, a general assembly at Þingvellir, which gave the island a common law without a king.</blockquote><a href="/state/icelandic-commonwealth">Icelandic Commonwealth</a></li></ul></section>
